B.dL0=new A.t("iconOnly",B.W,"Glyph-only \u2014 no visible label text. Requires `icon`; the `label` is kept as the button's Tooltip/semantics name (real a11y name, not just a visual hint) rather than dropped. Renders as an IconButton (or its filled/filledTonal/outlined variant, per `variant`) with a fixed 44x44 tap target regardless of `dense`. For a row of controls that can't all fit labeled (e.g. a reader action bar) \u2014 design-real-estate-efficiency, wikipick-0830ui.",B.f,null,!1)
B.dC9=new A.t("selectedWhen",B.S,'Render SELECTED (filled, or the selectedColor/selectedTextColor pinned tokens below when set) when the app state this button sets is the current one \u2014 for action-chain buttons, which setFilter highlighting never covered. Either { selected:"<table>", value:"<rowId>" } (a selectRow selection), { table, id, column, value } (a row field), or { mapLayer:"<glmap node id>", layer:"<layer id>" } (a glmap vector layer\'s ACTUAL live visibility, via BoardState.effectiveMapLayerVisible \u2014 the exact source of truth the map itself renders from, so an independent layer-toggle chip can never drift from what the map is showing).',B.f,null,!1)
B.dv4=new A.t("color",B.C,"Pin the button fill (hex) instead of inheriting the theme container role (secondaryContainer/primaryContainer), which is DERIVED from the seed color and can land on an unrelated hue.",B.f,null,!1)
B.dwM=new A.t("textColor",B.C,"Pin the label/icon color (hex) to pair with `color`.",B.f,null,!1)
B.dzC=new A.t("borderColor",B.C,"Pin an outline color (hex).",B.f,null,!1)
B.dMK=new A.t("selectedColor",B.C,"Pin the fill (hex) used when this button reads SELECTED (setFilter match or `selectedWhen`) \u2014 wins over `color` for that state only, so an independent toggle-chip row can show a real on/off state instead of every chip looking identical.",B.f,null,!1)
B.dKe=new A.t("selectedTextColor",B.C,"Pin the label/icon color (hex) used when this button reads SELECTED; pairs with `selectedColor`.",B.f,null,!1)
B.dtf=new A.t("darkColor",B.C,"`color` override used when the active brightness is dark; falls back to `color` when absent.",B.f,null,!1)
